{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"dystopian-bardlike-basil","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Problem\nThe complexity ratchet penalises the improvement it exists to encourage, and it does so in the\nworst possible way: the local gate passes and CI fails.\n\n`make lint` runs `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check`. That script states its own\ncontract at `scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py:64`:\n\n> ``stale>0`` alone is an allowed improvement (exit 0)\n\nA `stale` entry is defined at `:61` as \"key in baseline with no current diagnostic OR\n`current_score < ceiling`\" — i.e. **the function got simpler**. So `--check` exits 0, and `make lint`\nis green.\n\nCI disagrees. `tests/unit/test_complexity_baseline.py:123` asserts:\n\n```\nassert proc.stdout.strip() == f\"active={live} new=0 increased=0 stale=0\"\n```\n\n`stale=0` exactly. So the moment a change REDUCES the complexity of a function carrying a baseline\nceiling, `make lint` passes locally and CI returns `Verified -1`.\n\n## Evidence this is not theoretical\nGerrit change 1928 (`effective-cleansable-gull`, merged) hit exactly this. Its first patchset\nextracted a cluster out of `_validate_routing_entry`, which sat at its frozen ceiling of 17. Local\n`make lint` passed. CI failed `test_complexity_baseline.py` on three ubuntu shards. A real failure,\ncorrectly not rechecked.\n\n## Why this is worth fixing rather than documenting\nThe contributor is left without a sanctioned move:\n\n- Reducing complexity in a baselined function fails CI.\n- The drain command that would resolve it, `--update-stale`, is maintenance-only. AGENTS.md is\n  explicit that `--lock`/`--update-stale` are \"maintenance-only, not a contributor's escape hatch\".\n- So the remaining options are to make the change complexity-NEUTRAL, or not improve the function.\n\nThat is what 1928 actually did: it replaced the extraction with an UNCONDITIONAL helper call, which\nadds zero cyclomatic complexity, leaving the enclosing function's score unchanged. That was a fine\noutcome for that change — the diff shrank from 305/47 to 267/5 — but it was reached by steering\nAROUND the ratchet rather than with it. A gate whose existence is justified by reducing complexity\nshould not make reducing complexity the hard path.\n\nThe local/CI split compounds it. AGENTS.md already teaches that ambient-tool failures are\nenvironment errors rather than repository evidence; here the inverse happens — the local gate says\nthe tree is clean when CI will reject it. That erodes trust in `make lint` as a pre-push check,\nwhich is the entire reason it exists.\n\n## Investigation before choosing a remedy\nEstablish first whether the `stale=0` assertion is enforcing something real. The plausible intent is\n\"the COMMITTED baseline must be exactly in sync with the tree, with no drift\" — which is a coherent\ngoal, distinct from the script's general-purpose tolerance. If so, the defect is not the assertion\nbut the MISSING contributor path between the two.\n\nCandidate remedies, to be decided on evidence rather than assumed:\n- Make `make lint` match CI (fail on `stale>0`), so the local gate stops giving false assurance.\n  Honest, but leaves the contributor with no sanctioned way to land an improvement.\n- Give contributors a sanctioned drain for the narrow case where stale entries are caused BY THEIR\n  OWN change and every other counter is zero — a strictly-shrinking baseline edit is not the\n  escape-hatch risk the maintenance-only rule guards against.\n- Have the CI test tolerate `stale>0` when `new=0 increased=0`, and enforce sync separately in a\n  maintenance job rather than on the contributor's change.\n\nWhichever is chosen, the local and CI verdicts must agree.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `make lint` and CI's `test_complexity_baseline.py` return the SAME verdict for a tree in which\n      a baselined function's complexity has been REDUCED and no counter other than `stale` is\n      non-zero. Proven by a test exercising that exact state, not by inspection.\n- [ ] A contributor who legitimately reduces the complexity of a baselined function has a documented,\n      sanctioned path to land it that does NOT require an administrator override and does NOT\n      require making the change artificially complexity-neutral. The path is named in `AGENTS.md`.\n- [ ] The shrink-only guarantee is preserved: a change that ADDS a baselined symbol or INCREASES a\n      score still fails, and no remedy weakens that. Proven by a test.\n- [ ] `.github/complexity-baseline.json` remains locked against arbitrary contributor edits; any new\n      drain path is narrow, and refuses when `new>0` or `increased>0`.\n- [ ] `AGENTS.md`'s complexity-ratchet paragraph is updated to describe the actual contributor\n      workflow, including what to do when a change makes a baselined function simpler.\n\n## Provenance\nFiled 2026-08-19 after Gerrit 1928 failed CI on this exact path having passed `make lint`. Both the\nscript's tolerance (`check_complexity_baseline.py:61,64`) and the CI assertion\n(`tests/unit/test_complexity_baseline.py:123`) were read directly and are quoted above.\n","id":"d319-a094-4ee7-4549","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Complexity ratchet: make lint passes on stale>0 but CI requires stale=0, so reducing complexity fails CI"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787188324153293001,"uuid":"0eb8908e-c7c7-4918-99bc-02b9b230dfb2"}